Forward UDP packets received on mDNS broadcast channels to the main
listener.

matter.js opens N+1 sockets bound to 0.0.0.0:5353 inside the same
process (1 main listener + N broadcast channels for sending). All bind
with SO_REUSEADDR. The Linux kernel hashes a unicast 4-tuple to one of
these sockets deterministically; if the chosen socket is a broadcast
channel — which has no JS-level onData handler — libuv reads the packet
and silently drops it at the JS event layer, so DnssdNames never sees
it and CommissionableMdnsScanner never delivers the device.

Confirmed empirically on raspite: the device's only unicast response in
a 3 min discovery window arrived on broadcast-channel-v6, never on
listener-v6, and commissioning failed with "No commissionable device
was discovered".

This patch wires every broadcast channel's inbound to the listener that
onMessage() registered, so packets are processed regardless of which
socket the kernel picked. Also leaves diagnostic probes in place so
behavior can be re-verified after deploy.

Upstream issue: should be filed against project-chip/matter.js
(@matter/general/src/net/udp/UdpMulticastServer.ts).
